# Database schema conventions

## Primary keys

Every table has an `id` uuid primary key defaulting to `gen_random_uuid()`.

```sql
id uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
```

## Fully qualified names

Always schema-qualify tables, references, and indexes. Never rely on
`search_path` in migrations.

```sql
-- ✅ GOOD
CREATE TABLE lbry.city (...);
CREATE INDEX city_state_id_idx ON lbry.city (state_id);

-- ❌ BAD
CREATE TABLE city (...);
```

## Text columns use citext

Enable the extension once, then use `citext` (case-insensitive) instead of
`text` for all text columns.

```sql
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS citext WITH SCHEMA lbry;
name lbry.citext NOT NULL,
```

## Foreign keys

- Reference the fully qualified target table.
- Do NOT use `ON DELETE CASCADE` / `ON DELETE SET NULL`; leave the default
  `NO ACTION` so deletes are explicit.
- Create an index on every FK column (Postgres does not add one automatically).

```sql
-- ✅ GOOD
state_id uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES lbry.state (id),
...
CREATE INDEX city_state_id_idx ON lbry.city (state_id);

-- ❌ BAD
state_id uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES lbry.state (id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
```

## Timestamps

Every table has `created_at` and `updated_at`.

```sql
created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
updated_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
```

## Constraints

- Natural keys (codes, emails, isbns, barcodes) get a `UNIQUE` constraint.
- Join tables carry a composite `UNIQUE (a_id, b_id)` plus an index per FK.
